le=\"top:-1.8023em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">8.05</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">−</span><span class=\"mord mtight\">24</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-0.6023em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">4.00</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:2.7577em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"arraycolsep\" style=\"width:0.5em;\"></span><span class=\"vertical-separator\" style=\"height:6.0153em;border-right-width:0.04em;border-right-style:solid;margin:0 -0.02em;vertical-align:-2.7577em;\"></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-2.5em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:5.2577em;\"></span><span class=\"hline\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.7em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:5.2577em;\"></span><span class=\"hline\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-4.9051em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:5.2577em;\"></span><span class=\"hline\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-6.1102em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:5.2577em;\"></span><span class=\"hline\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-7.3153em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:5.2577em;\"></span><span class=\"hline\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-8.5153em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:5.2577em;\"></span><span class=\"hline\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:2.7577em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></div></div></div>",
       "mime": "x-application/wolfram-language-html"
      },
      {
       "data": "Out[385]= \\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline\\varepsilon  & \\text{max ‖u‖} & \\text{max ‖c$\\cdot$h‖ on HW\\_[5,4,3]} & \\text{‖c\\_T(4,2)$\\cdot$h\\_[4,4,4]‖} & \\text{‖x0‖ (two-site)}\\\\\\hline\\frac{1}{10000} & 2.50 & 4.92\\times 10^{-15} & 3.36\\times 10^{-20} & 8.05\\times 10^{-16}\\\\\\hline\\frac{1}{100000} & 2.50 & 4.92\\times 10^{-18} & 3.36\\times 10^{-24} & 8.05\\times 10^{-20}\\\\\\hline\\frac{1}{1000000} & 2.50 & 4.92\\times 10^{-21} & 3.36\\times 10^{-28} & 8.05\\times 10^{-24}\\\\\\hline\\text{$\\varepsilon$-power (last two $\\varepsilon$)} &  & 3.00 & 4.00 & 4.00\\\\\\hline\\end{array}",
       "mime": "text/plain"
      }
     ],
     "id": "f34e0bc6-2f46-4dba-828e-e9430cf69272"
    }
   ],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "### Result (2026-10-02, P1)\n- **The localized data are not blind to $[5,4,3]$ and $[4,4,4]$.**\n  - Site-1 $T_{(3,2)}$ and $T_{(4,2)}$ at $\\theta_1-4h$ have components in site-1 weight $(1,2,3)$, and $T_{(4,2)}$ also in $(0,2,4)$.\n  - The direct overlaps $c\\cdot h$ are nonzero: $\\sim8\\times10^{-3}$ on $[5,4,3]$ and $8\\times10^{-4}$ on $[4,4,4]$ at the finite test twist.\n  - At small twist they scale exactly like $\\varepsilon^3$ ($[5,4,3]$) and $\\varepsilon^4$ ($[4,4,4]$), while the localized vectors stay $O(1)$ ($\\|u\\|\\approx2.5$).\n  - Site-1 $T_{(1,1)},T_{(2,2)}$ and the site-2 objects $t_2(\\theta_2+h)$, $T_{(2,2)}(\\theta_2)$ have zero overlap with these two HW spaces. For comparison, on $[6,4,2]$ only $T_{(1,1)}$ and site-2 $t_2(\\theta_2+h)$ have zero overlap.\n- **What `L2-420-localization-EH.wb` cell 14 actually showed.** It printed the overlap singular values of $\\mathrm{HW}_\\mu$ with the numerical null space at 3 digits, as \"1.000\". That is consistent with HW vectors lying *almost* in the null space, because their overlaps are $\\varepsilon^{3,4}$-suppressed at $\\varepsilon=10^{-6}$. It is not an exact zero. So the conclusion \"structurally invisible, no $\\varepsilon$-order or $w$ can help\" is **wrong**. The finite-$\\varepsilon$ reconstruction failure itself was real. Its cause, deep $\\varepsilon$-suppression versus a genuine null direction with HW content, is open.\n- **Open.** Run the clean-combination analysis (as in `Minimal-ingredients-310-EH.wb`, Section 8) for $[4,2,0]^2$, to see whether $[5,4,3]$ and $[4,4,4]$ can be extracted at leading order.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  }
